    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d370">

                <pb facs="0324" n="318" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg318"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.115/616: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul at Hankow</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Jarvis</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-04-21T20:00:00-05:00">April 21, 1939—8 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>34. Your 93, April 19, 5 p.m.<anchor n="52" xml:id="d370-f1"/> The Department
                    assumes from your telegram that you do not anticipate any physical attempt to
                    prevent departure from Hankow of Americans who have not obtained Japanese
                    “permission”. The Department also assumes that any Americans who may leave
                    Hankow without such “permission” and who may decide to return to Hankow have in
                    mind the practical difficulties that might attend their return landing or
                    sojourn.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking, Peiping, and Shanghai.</p>
                <p>Shanghai please repeat to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d370-f1" n="52" xml:id="d370-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that Americans were planning to depart aboard American
                    gunboats without Japanese “permission” (393.115/615).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
